1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,634
You're sinking.

2
00:00:01,654 --> 00:00:02,274
Above you,

3
00:00:02,294 --> 00:00:04,263
about eleven kilometres of water.

4
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:04,817
Around you,

5
00:00:04,817 --> 00:00:06,151
total darkness.

6
00:00:06,171 --> 00:00:07,078
And a pressure more than

7
00:00:07,078 --> 00:00:08,248
a thousand times stronger than

8
00:00:08,248 --> 00:00:08,931
up here,

9
00:00:08,951 --> 00:00:10,274
ready to crush anything not

10
00:00:10,274 --> 00:00:10,925
built for it.

11
00:00:11,925 --> 00:00:13,585
Welcome to the Mariana Trench,

12
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:14,819
in the Pacific Ocean.

13
00:00:14,839 --> 00:00:16,069
Its deepest spot,

14
00:00:16,089 --> 00:00:17,159
Challenger Deep,

15
00:00:17,179 --> 00:00:20,837
is about 10,935 metres down.

16
00:00:20,857 --> 00:00:22,227
Drop Mount Everest in,

17
00:00:22,247 --> 00:00:23,345
and its peak would still

18
00:00:23,345 --> 00:00:25,162
be about two kilometres underwater.

19
00:00:28,162 --> 00:00:29,500
How did such a hole form?

20
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,008
Here, one huge piece of

21
00:00:31,008 --> 00:00:32,370
Earth's crust,

22
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:33,800
a tectonic plate,

23
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:35,700
dives underneath another one,

24
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:37,462
and bends the sea floor down.

25
00:00:37,482 --> 00:00:38,597
The trench it leaves is

26
00:00:38,597 --> 00:00:41,439
about 2,500 kilometres long.

27
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:42,699
A deep scar,

28
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:44,774
hidden under the Pacific.

29
00:00:44,794 --> 00:00:45,754
As you sink,

30
00:00:45,774 --> 00:00:47,309
the sunlight fades fast.

31
00:00:47,329 --> 00:00:48,348
Most of it is gone

32
00:00:48,348 --> 00:00:50,478
by about 200 metres.

33
00:00:50,498 --> 00:00:52,178
Below about a thousand metres,

34
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:53,148
it's pitch black.

35
00:00:53,168 --> 00:00:54,227
Scientists call it the

36
00:00:54,227 --> 00:00:55,139
midnight zone.

37
00:00:55,159 --> 00:00:56,069
And you're not even a

38
00:00:56,069 --> 00:00:56,945
tenth of the way down.

39
00:00:57,945 --> 00:01:00,006
But it's not completely dark everywhere.

40
00:01:00,026 --> 00:01:01,457
Many deep-sea animals make

41
00:01:01,457 --> 00:01:02,296
their own light,

42
00:01:02,316 --> 00:01:04,072
with chemistry inside their bodies.

43
00:01:04,092 --> 00:01:06,089
It's called bioluminescence.

44
00:01:06,109 --> 00:01:07,499
Flashes, glowing lures,

45
00:01:07,519 --> 00:01:09,002
even light used as camouflage.

46
00:01:10,003 --> 00:01:11,900
Now the real problem.

47
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:13,016
Ever felt your ears hurt

48
00:01:13,016 --> 00:01:13,919
at the bottom of a

49
00:01:13,919 --> 00:01:14,814
swimming pool?

50
00:01:14,834 --> 00:01:15,914
That's just a few metres

51
00:01:15,914 --> 00:01:16,521
of water.

52
00:01:16,541 --> 00:01:17,343
At the bottom of the

53
00:01:17,343 --> 00:01:18,768
trench, the water pressure is

54
00:01:18,768 --> 00:01:20,048
more than a thousand times

55
00:01:20,048 --> 00:01:21,430
what you feel right now.

56
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:22,456
That's about eight tons

57
00:01:22,456 --> 00:01:24,010
pressing on every square inch.

58
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:25,167
Roughly like a big elephant

59
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:26,996
standing on your thumb.

60
00:01:27,016 --> 00:01:28,358
Humans first made it in

61
00:01:28,358 --> 00:01:30,579
January 1960.

62
00:01:30,599 --> 00:01:31,359
Two explorers,

63
00:01:31,359 --> 00:01:32,709
Don Walsh and Jacques Piccard,

64
00:01:32,729 --> 00:01:33,724
sank to the bottom in

65
00:01:33,724 --> 00:01:35,119
a vessel called the Trieste.

66
00:01:35,139 --> 00:01:36,401
The trip down took almost

67
00:01:36,401 --> 00:01:36,945
five hours.

68
00:01:37,945 --> 00:01:39,075
On the way down,

69
00:01:39,095 --> 00:01:40,241
an outer window cracked with

70
00:01:40,241 --> 00:01:41,119
a loud bang.

71
00:01:41,139 --> 00:01:42,956
They kept going anyway.

72
00:01:42,976 --> 00:01:43,992
They spent about twenty

73
00:01:43,992 --> 00:01:45,256
minutes on the bottom,

74
00:01:45,276 --> 00:01:46,646
looking out into the dark,

75
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:47,705
and then began the long

76
00:01:47,705 --> 00:01:49,077
climb back up to the surface.

77
00:01:50,077 --> 00:01:51,620
Here's something wild.

78
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:52,602
Fish can't live all the

79
00:01:52,602 --> 00:01:53,277
way down.

80
00:01:53,297 --> 00:01:54,970
The deepest fish ever filmed

81
00:01:54,970 --> 00:01:56,577
was a small snailfish,

82
00:01:56,597 --> 00:01:58,967
at 8,336 metres,

83
00:01:58,987 --> 00:02:00,342
off Japan.

84
00:02:00,362 --> 00:02:01,918
Scientists think fish can't

85
00:02:01,918 --> 00:02:03,649
survive much deeper than about

86
00:02:03,649 --> 00:02:06,962
8,200 to 8,400 metres.

87
00:02:08,312 --> 00:02:08,912
Why?

88
00:02:08,932 --> 00:02:10,109
To protect their cells from

89
00:02:10,109 --> 00:02:10,902
the pressure,

90
00:02:10,922 --> 00:02:11,932
deep fish load up on

91
00:02:11,932 --> 00:02:13,162
a special molecule.

92
00:02:13,182 --> 00:02:14,572
But the deeper they go,

93
00:02:14,592 --> 00:02:15,642
the more they need.

94
00:02:15,662 --> 00:02:17,122
Past a certain depth,

95
00:02:17,142 --> 00:02:18,354
scientists think their bodies

96
00:02:18,354 --> 00:02:19,938
would pull in too much water.

97
00:02:19,958 --> 00:02:20,968
So below that line:

98
00:02:20,988 --> 00:02:21,614
no fish.

99
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:24,103
But life doesn't stop.

100
00:02:24,123 --> 00:02:25,673
At the very bottom,

101
00:02:25,693 --> 00:02:26,693
scientists have found

102
00:02:26,693 --> 00:02:27,968
shrimp-like scavengers called

103
00:02:27,968 --> 00:02:29,400
amphipods, giant single-celled

104
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:31,083
blobs called xenophyophores,

105
00:02:31,103 --> 00:02:32,744
and see-through sea cucumbers.

106
00:02:32,764 --> 00:02:34,780
Life, eleven kilometres down.

107
00:02:36,129 --> 00:02:37,795
One of those amphipods even

108
00:02:37,795 --> 00:02:39,413
makes its own special enzyme

109
00:02:39,413 --> 00:02:41,158
that turns wood into sugar.

110
00:02:41,178 --> 00:02:42,269
So when bits of sunken

111
00:02:42,269 --> 00:02:43,318
plants drift down,

112
00:02:43,338 --> 00:02:45,002
dinner is served.

113
00:02:45,022 --> 00:02:46,374
And here's the sad part.

114
00:02:46,394 --> 00:02:47,830
A plastic bag was spotted

115
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:49,594
almost eleven kilometres down,

116
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:52,841
at 10,898 metres.

117
00:02:52,861 --> 00:02:54,156
And amphipods from the trench

118
00:02:54,156 --> 00:02:55,319
were found carrying human

119
00:02:55,319 --> 00:02:57,087
pollution, chemicals made in

120
00:02:57,087 --> 00:02:58,074
factories far away.

121
00:02:59,074 --> 00:02:59,944
Since then,

122
00:02:59,964 --> 00:03:00,971
more explorers have followed.

123
00:03:00,991 --> 00:03:02,341
In 2012,

124
00:03:02,361 --> 00:03:03,902
filmmaker James Cameron made

125
00:03:03,902 --> 00:03:05,531
the first solo dive,

126
00:03:05,551 --> 00:03:08,625
reaching about 10,908 metres.

127
00:03:08,645 --> 00:03:10,305
In 2019,

128
00:03:10,325 --> 00:03:11,485
Victor Vescovo's sub,

129
00:03:11,505 --> 00:03:12,125
Limiting Factor,

130
00:03:12,145 --> 00:03:14,124
made several dives there.

131
00:03:14,144 --> 00:03:15,624
And in 2020,

132
00:03:15,644 --> 00:03:16,665
former astronaut Kathy

133
00:03:16,665 --> 00:03:17,742
Sullivan became the first

134
00:03:17,742 --> 00:03:19,518
woman to reach the bottom.

135
00:03:19,538 --> 00:03:20,748
She's also the first person

136
00:03:20,748 --> 00:03:21,814
to have walked in space

137
00:03:21,814 --> 00:03:22,808
and been to the deepest

138
00:03:22,808 --> 00:03:23,859
point of the ocean.

139
00:03:23,879 --> 00:03:24,689
From the top of the

140
00:03:24,689 --> 00:03:25,942
sky, to the bottom of

141
00:03:25,942 --> 00:03:27,263
the sea.

142
00:03:27,283 --> 00:03:28,851
So let's recap.

143
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:30,722
About eleven kilometres of water.

144
00:03:30,742 --> 00:03:31,930
More than a thousand times

145
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:32,589
the pressure.

146
00:03:32,609 --> 00:03:33,159
No light,

147
00:03:33,179 --> 00:03:34,519
no fish at the bottom,

148
00:03:34,539 --> 00:03:35,229
but still life,

149
00:03:35,249 --> 00:03:35,855
and sadly,

150
00:03:35,855 --> 00:03:36,984
still our trash.

151
00:03:37,004 --> 00:03:38,098
So could you dive there?

152
00:03:38,118 --> 00:03:39,784
Only inside a very strong

153
00:03:39,784 --> 00:03:41,625
sub, built just for this.

154
00:03:41,645 --> 00:03:42,515
Without one,

155
00:03:42,535 --> 00:03:44,365
no diving suit could protect you.

156
00:03:44,385 --> 00:03:45,275
Next time,

157
00:03:45,295 --> 00:03:46,535
a smaller question with a

158
00:03:46,535 --> 00:03:47,555
huge answer:

159
00:03:47,575 --> 00:03:48,880
what if every insect on

160
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:49,704
Earth disappeared?

161
00:03:51,104 --> 00:03:52,506
Would you get in the submarine?

162
00:03:52,526 --> 00:03:53,799
Tell me in the comments.

163
00:03:53,819 --> 00:03:54,929
Next time:

164
00:03:54,949 --> 00:03:56,348
what if every insect on

165
00:03:56,348 --> 00:03:57,831
Earth suddenly vanished?

166
00:03:57,851 --> 00:03:59,071
You might be surprised who'd

167
00:03:59,071 --> 00:04:00,001
miss them most.

168
00:04:00,021 --> 00:04:00,725
Subscribe.
